From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dzulfikar Ats Tsauri <99225357+dzulfikar08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:40:09 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] hw/net/can/ctucan: report fault confinement state, become
 error-active

The model never left the reset fault confinement state: reads of the
EWL/ERP/FAULT_STATE and REC/TEC registers returned 0 and INT_STAT[FCSI]
was never set. The Linux ctucanfd driver sets the interface state to
STOPPED in ctucan_chip_start() and expects the first FCSI interrupt
after enabling the core to move it to ERROR-ACTIVE, reading the state
from FAULT_STATE. With the model, "ip -details link show" therefore
reports "state STOPPED" while frames are sent and received, and an FCSI
alone would not help, since FAULT_STATE reads as 0 and the driver warns
"Invalid error state".

Model what the IP core does (CTU CAN FD datasheet register map and RTL,
fault_confinement_fsm.vhd and protocol_control_fsm.vhd):

- MODE[RST] sets EW_LIMIT to 96, ERP_LIMIT to 128 and FAULT_STATE to
  bus-off, with REC and TEC zero (the register reset values).
- The CAN core is held in reset while SETTINGS[ENA] is 0, so clearing
  ENA returns the node to bus-off with cleared error counters.
- When ENA is set, the core integrates to the bus and, coming from
  bus-off, becomes error-active and raises FCSI. The model has no bit
  timing, so this happens at once.
- EWL/ERP/FAULT_STATE and REC/TEC are readable.

The model does not emulate bus errors, so the node stays error-active
until it is disabled or reset. Both registers were already part of the
migration state.

Tested with Linux 6.18 (ctucanfd_pci) on qemu-system-aarch64 (virt):
after "ip link set can0 up" the driver logs an FCSI interrupt
(ISR = 0x00000010) and "state changes from CAN_STATE_STOPPED to
CAN_STATE_ERROR_ACTIVE", and "ip -details link show can0" reports
"state ERROR-ACTIVE"; after "ip link set can0 down" it reports STOPPED,
and the next "up" raises FCSI again.

Upstream-Status: Pending [prepared for qemu-devel, not sent yet: docs/upstream/]
Signed-off-by: Dzulfikar Ats Tsauri <99225357+dzulfikar08@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 hw/net/can/ctucan_core.c | 50 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/net/can/ctucan_core.c b/hw/net/can/ctucan_core.c
index 6bd99c4..631731d 100644
--- a/hw/net/can/ctucan_core.c
+++ b/hw/net/can/ctucan_core.c
@@ -187,6 +187,36 @@ static void ctucan_update_txnf(CtuCanCoreState *s)
     s->status.s.txnf = txnf;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Fault confinement. While SETTINGS[ENA] is 0 the CAN core is held in reset:
+ * the node is bus-off and both error counters are zero. After the core is
+ * enabled it integrates to the bus and, coming from bus-off, becomes
+ * error-active, clears REC and TEC and raises FCSI. The model has no bit
+ * timing, so integration completes at once, and it never sees bus errors,
+ * so the node stays error-active until it is disabled or reset.
+ */
+static void ctucan_fault_confinement_reset(CtuCanCoreState *s)
+{
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.era = 0;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.erp = 0;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.bof = 1;
+    s->rec_tec.u32 = 0;
+}
+
+static void ctucan_set_err_active(CtuCanCoreState *s)
+{
+    union ctu_can_fd_int_stat int_stat;
+
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.era = 1;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.erp = 0;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.bof = 0;
+    s->rec_tec.u32 = 0;
+
+    int_stat.u32 = 0;
+    int_stat.s.fcsi = 1;
+    s->int_stat.u32 |= int_stat.u32 & ~s->int_mask.u32;
+}
+
 void ctucan_hardware_reset(CtuCanCoreState *s)
 {
     DPRINTF("Hardware reset in progress!!!\n");
@@ -229,6 +259,11 @@ void ctucan_hardware_reset(CtuCanCoreState *s)
     s->rx_fr_ctr.u32 = 0;
     s->tx_fr_ctr.u32 = 0;
 
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.u32 = 0;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.ew_limit = 96;
+    s->ewl_erp_fault_state.s.erp_limit = 128;
+    ctucan_fault_confinement_reset(s);
+
     s->yolo_reg.s.yolo_val = 3735928559;
 
     qemu_irq_lower(s->irq);
@@ -310,12 +345,21 @@ void ctucan_mem_write(CtuCanCoreState *s, hwaddr addr, uint64_t val,
     } else {
         switch (addr & ~3) {
         case CTU_CAN_FD_MODE:
+        {
+            bool was_enabled = s->mode_settings.s.ena;
+
             s->mode_settings.u32 = (uint32_t)val;
             if (s->mode_settings.s.rst) {
                 ctucan_hardware_reset(s);
                 s->mode_settings.s.rst = 0;
             }
+            if (!was_enabled && s->mode_settings.s.ena) {
+                ctucan_set_err_active(s);
+            } else if (was_enabled && !s->mode_settings.s.ena) {
+                ctucan_fault_confinement_reset(s);
+            }
             break;
+        }
         case CTU_CAN_FD_COMMAND:
         {
             union ctu_can_fd_command command;
@@ -493,6 +537,12 @@ uint64_t ctucan_mem_read(CtuCanCoreState *s, hwaddr addr, unsigned size)
             val = 0;
         }
         break;
+    case CTU_CAN_FD_EWL:
+        val = s->ewl_erp_fault_state.u32;
+        break;
+    case CTU_CAN_FD_REC:
+        val = s->rec_tec.u32;
+        break;
     case CTU_CAN_FD_TX_STATUS:
         val = s->tx_status.u32;
         break;
